The Open Systems Interconnection (OSI) model is a conceptual framework that divides network communication into seven distinct layers to help us understand how hardware and software work together
. To easily remember the layers from bottom to top (Layer 1 to Layer 7), you can use the mnemonic "Please Do Not Throw Sausage Pizza Away."
.
Here is an explanation of each layer, working from the top (closest to the user) down to the bottom (the physical hardware), along with real-world examples:
7. Application Layer This layer serves as the direct interface between the network and your end-user applications
. It is not the application itself, but rather the protocols that allow the application to access network resources
.
Real-world example: When you open a web browser and type in a URL, the browser acts as the application using the HTTP protocol to prepare your request for the network
. Another example is an email client, like Outlook, using the SMTP protocol to send a message
.
6. Presentation Layer Also known as the syntax layer, this step is responsible for translating, formatting, compressing, and encrypting data so that it can be understood by the receiving application
.
Real-world example: If you are sending a highly sensitive email to a colleague, the presentation layer encrypts the email data to ensure it is secure during transmission
. It is also responsible for handling familiar file formats like JPEG images or video files, so they display correctly
.
5. Session Layer The session layer establishes, manages, and terminates connections—known as "sessions"—between local and remote applications
. It tracks these conversations and ensures they stay open long enough for data to be exchanged
.
Real-world example: When a user in New York emails a colleague in London, the session layer opens and manages the connection between the sender's email server and the receiver's email server, keeping it active until the entire email is successfully transferred
.
4. Transport Layer Data from the upper layers can be very large, so the transport layer breaks it down into manageable chunks called "segments" (for TCP) or "datagrams" (for UDP)
. It provides end-to-end communication, assigns port numbers to identify which application the data belongs to, and handles flow control and error recovery
.
Real-world example: When you are loading a webpage, this layer uses the Transmission Control Protocol (TCP) to ensure all segments arrive reliably and in order
. Alternatively, if you are playing a fast-paced online video game or streaming video, this layer might use the User Datagram Protocol (UDP) for faster transmission, sacrificing perfect reliability for speed
.
3. Network Layer This layer is responsible for routing data across different networks, turning segments into "packets" by adding logical addressing (IP addresses) to the data header
. It determines the best physical path for the data to take to reach its final destination
.
Real-world example: When you send an email overseas, the network layer attaches the source IP address (your computer) and the destination IP address (the London server) so that the internet's routers know exactly how to forward the packet across multiple networks to reach the correct country
.
2. Data Link Layer While the network layer gets data across the globe, the data link layer is strictly responsible for node-to-node delivery on a single local network segment
. It packages packets into "frames," adds physical MAC (Media Access Control) addresses, and provides error detection
.
Real-world example: When your computer wants to send data out to the internet, the data link layer uses the Ethernet protocol to establish a logical link with your home router, wrapping the data in a frame addressed specifically to the router's MAC address
.
1. Physical Layer The bottom layer defines the physical and hardware elements of the network connection
. It is responsible for converting the data frames into raw binary bits (1s and 0s) and transmitting them as signals over the physical medium
.
Real-world example: Converting data into pulsing light to travel down underwater fiber-optic cables, encoding it as electrical signals over a copper wire, or broadcasting it as radio frequencies from a Wi-Fi access point
.

Network to subnet: 192.168.1.0/24
Requirement: Divide it into at least 6 subnets (a frequent task — enough for departments, VLANs, etc., but not too many to waste addresses).
To create at least 6 subnets from a /24 network, we borrow 3 bits from the host portion (because 2³ = 8 subnets > 6; 2² = 4 subnets is not enough).
New subnet mask: /27 (255.255.255.224)
This gives us:

8 possible subnets (we'll use the first 6 for the example)
32 addresses per subnet (30 usable hosts per subnet after subtracting network + broadcast)

How I Calculated It
I used a reliable online subnet calculator (subnet-calculator.com and cross-checked with calculator.net and mxtoolbox.com).
Steps in the calculator:

Enter IP: 192.168.1.0
Enter original mask: /24 (or 255.255.255.0)
Request: maximum subnets = 8 (or new mask bits = 27, or subnet bits = 3)
Calculate

Documented Results
Original Network

Address: 192.168.1.0
Mask: 255.255.255.0 (/24)
Total addresses: 256
Usable hosts: 254

After Subnetting (into /27 subnets)

New mask: 255.255.255.224 (/27)
Subnet increment: 32 (256 ÷ 8 = 32)
Subnets created: 8
Addresses per subnet: 32
Usable hosts per subnet: 30 (32 – 2)

